.TH HDA 4 "August 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
hda \- Intel High Definition Audio controller driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B device hda
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B hda
driver supports audio controllers conforming to the
.I Intel High Definition Audio Specification
revision 1.0a \(em PCI class 0x04, subclass 0x03, the successor to
AC'97.  It attaches to any such controller found on the PCI bus and
registers the first codec with a usable analog output path as an
.BR audio (4)
device, published as
.B /dev/audio0
and
.BR /dev/dsp0 .
.PP
Playback only.  The driver programs one output stream descriptor; it
does not implement capture, so
.B AUDIO_GETPROPS
reports
.B AUDIO_PROP_PLAYBACK
alone.
.SS Controller
All registers live in a single MMIO BAR.  At attach the driver stops
every DMA engine, resets the controller, waits the 521 microseconds the
specification requires for codec enumeration, and brings up the CORB and
RIRB command rings at whatever size the controller advertises in
.B CORBSIZE
and
.BR RIRBSIZE .
.PP
Output stream descriptors follow the input ones in the register block, so
the descriptor driven is index
.IR ISS ,
not zero.  Playback uses a cyclic ring of 32 buffer-descriptor entries of
4 KiB each, refilled from a 256 KiB software FIFO by the completion
interrupt.
.PP
An interrupt line is mandatory.  The ring is refilled from the completion
path, so a controller with no usable IRQ is not attached rather than
being published as a device that would stop after its first few buffers.
.SS Codec
Every codec reported in
.B STATESTS
is tried in turn until one yields an output path.  Within a codec the
driver locates the audio function group, collects the analog output
converters and the output pins that
.B CONFIG_DEFAULT
reports as physically connected, ranks the pins (speaker, then line out,
then headphone, then anything else), and selects the best-ranked pin that
some converter can actually reach \(em directly or through one
intermediate mixer or selector.
.PP
Digital widgets are skipped.  An HDMI or DisplayPort path additionally
needs digital converter control, channel mapping and ELD handling, none
of which is implemented, so such a pin would appear configured and stay
silent.
.PP
Amplifiers along the chosen path are unmuted and set to their 0 dB step.
Amplifier parameters come from the widget only when it sets
.RB \(lq "Amp Param Override" \(rq;
otherwise the function group's defaults apply.  Where the pin reports the
capability, its external amplifier (EAPD) is enabled.
.SS Formats
Linear PCM, 8/16/20/24/32 bits, 1 to 16 channels, at the rates the
selected converter advertises in its
.RB \(lq "Supported PCM Size, Rates" \(rq
parameter \(em a subset of 8, 11.025, 16, 22.05, 32, 44.1, 48, 88.2, 96,
176.4 and 192 kHz.  48 kHz is mandatory for all codecs.
.PP
A rate, sample width or channel count the converter does not claim is
rejected with
.B EINVAL
rather than programmed and rendered at the wrong speed.  Changing the
format stops the stream and discards anything still queued, which was
recorded in the previous format.
.PP
Encoding is not converted.  The framework accepts
.BR AUDIO_ENCODING_ULAW ,
.BR AUDIO_ENCODING_ALAW
and the unsigned and big-endian variants, but this driver hands the bytes
to the controller unchanged, so only
.B AUDIO_ENCODING_SLINEAR_LE
renders correctly.
.SH DEVICE NODES
.TP
.B /dev/audio0 , /dev/audioctl0
Sun/NetBSD-compatible sample and control devices; see
.BR audio (4).
.TP
.B /dev/dsp0
OSS-compatible sample device.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B hda: \fIvendor\fB:\fIdevice\fB oss=\fIn\fB iss=\fIn\fB codecs=\fImask\fB cad=\fIn\fB vid=\fIid\fB corb=\fIn\fB rirb=\fIn
Attach succeeded.  Reports the PCI IDs, the output and input stream
counts from
.BR GCAP ,
the codec presence mask, the codec address in use, its vendor ID, and the
negotiated command-ring sizes.
.TP
.B hda: codec \fIn\fB afg=\fInid\fB dac=\fInid\fB pin=\fInid\fB tag=\fIn\fB chan=\fIn\fB pcm=\fIcaps
The selected output path and the converter's channel count and PCM
capability word.
.TP
.B hda: no codec detected
.B STATESTS
was empty after reset.  No codec is attached to the link, or the
controller never left reset.
.TP
.B hda: no analog output widgets (dacs=\fIn\fB pins=\fIn\fB)
The codec has no analog converter, or no output pin that
.B CONFIG_DEFAULT
reports as connected.  Common on display-audio-only controllers.
.TP
.B hda: no explicit route to any pin; assuming pin \fIn\fB is hard-wired to dac \fIn
No connection list joins any candidate pair within one intermediate
widget.  A pin with a single hard-wired source and no connection list is
legitimate, so the best-ranked pair is used anyway.
.TP
.B hda: no usable IRQ line; not attaching
.BR pci_get_irq (9)
returned no line.  See
.B Controller
above.
.TP
.B hda: \fIn\fB verb timeout(s) during configuration
The codec failed to answer within the per-verb budget.  The graph was
configured from incomplete information and playback may be silent.
.TP
.B hda: \fIn\fB FIFO underrun(s), \fIn\fB descriptor error(s)
Reported on close.  Underruns mean the software FIFO was not refilled in
time; a descriptor error is fatal to the stream and forces a restart.
.TP
.B hda: could not enable PCIe snoop on \fIvendor\fB; DMA may read stale data
An ATI, AMD or NVIDIA controller refused the snoop-enable bit.  The
driver's DMA memory is cacheable, so the controller may fetch stale ring
contents.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audio (4),
.BR uac (4),
.BR audioctl (1)
.PP
.I High Definition Audio Specification, Revision 1.0a
(Intel Corporation, 17 June 2010).
.SH HISTORY
The
.B hda
driver first appeared in Substrate alongside the
.BR audio (4)
framework.  It was audited against the 1.0a specification and the
FreeBSD and NetBSD drivers in August 2026.
.SH BUGS
Capture is not implemented.
.PP
HDMI and DisplayPort output are not implemented; digital pins are
ignored during path selection.
.PP
Only the first controller-supported analog path is used.  There is no
mixer control beyond the framework's gain and balance, and no runtime
selection between speaker and headphone pins \(em jack-presence
unsolicited responses are discarded rather than acted on.
.PP
DMA buffers are ordinary cacheable memory.  This is correct on Intel
controllers and on any controller whose PCIe snoop bit could be set, but
the kernel cannot currently allocate uncacheable memory for the cases
where neither holds.
